\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Matched SUM159 lineages reveal environment-dependent ploidy selection.} \textbf  {(A)} Overview of the matched SUM159 near-diploid and near-tetraploid lineages analyzed \textit  {in vitro} and \textit  {in vivo} on comparable experimental time axes. The \textit  {in vitro} portion shows control and O1/O2 hypoxia-anoxia passage histories, with target O$_2$ encoded by blue intensity and measurement events marked for flow cytometry and karyotyping. The \textit  {in vivo} portion shows tumor-burden trajectories for the fitted untreated harvest mice, with terminal chromosome-number and histologic necrosis measurements indicated at harvest.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:iteration1-matched-sum159-data-streams}{{1}{8}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Model overview: resource limitation can impose opposing pressures on ploidy evolution.} \textbf  {(A)} Resource limitation is represented as a model axis that can reshape ploidy through competing mechanisms. \textbf  {(B)} High ploidy is costly under resource stress through growth suppression and death. \textbf  {(C)} Stress can increase CIN/WGD pressure, generating chromosome-number variation. \textbf  {(D)} High ploidy can buffer otherwise lethal chromosome missegregation. \textbf  {(E)} Growth, death, CIN/WGD transitions, and post-missegregation survival define the fixed-O$_2$ transition matrix; its dominant eigenvector gives the long-term chromosome-state distribution and mean ploidy.}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:iteration1-model-overview}{{2}{9}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {In vitro ploidy reshaping occurs without strong high-ploidy elimination.} \textbf  {(A)} The separate \textit  {in vitro} fit tracks passage growth, chromosome-count observations, and model-predicted live/dead burden components across 2N and 4N control and oxygen-deprived branches. \textbf  {(B)} The predicted chromosome-state distribution across passages shows the oxygen-deprived 2N branch moving through a transient high-ploidy/WGD-like expansion, followed by chromosome-loss-derived viable descendants and a shift toward lower-ploidy states. \textbf  {(C)} The fitted post-missegregation survival function increases with chromosome number, allowing chromosome-loss daughters from higher-ploidy mothers to remain viable more often than comparable daughters from lower-ploidy mothers. \textbf  {(D)} The model-implied nonviable daughter fraction plotted against missegregation rate shows how chromosome-loss opportunity and ploidy-dependent survival jointly determine which chromosome-segregation-error products remain viable across reference ploidy states. \textbf  {(E)} Passage-end predictions along the terminal severe-deprivation 4N lineage show a decline in mean chromosome number from 84.3 to 80.8 while model-predicted direct hypoxia-origin dead burden remains at or below 1.72\% of total burden. The larger dead-buffer component records nonviable chromosome-transition products, including out-of-grid boundary routing, and is distinct from direct hypoxia-origin killing of the high-ploidy population.}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:iteration1-invitro-ploidy-reshaping}{{3}{10}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {In vivo fit-derived resource regimes and parameter landscapes.} \textbf  {(A)} Fitted effective O$_2$ trajectories from the separate \textit  {in vivo} fit provide a latent model-implied resource axis rather than direct oxygen measurements. \textbf  {(B-D)} One-feature AUC screens identify parameters and derived features that best separate lower- from higher-ploidy fixed-O$_2$ outcomes at low, intermediate, and high reference O$_2$. \textbf  {(E)} Prior-scaled fitted parameter positions for baseline missegregation and oxygen-response shape differ descriptively across three \textit  {in vivo} solution clusters. \textbf  {(F)} Pooled t-SNE visualization of initial and best-fit \textit  {in vivo}/\textit  {in vitro} parameter samples, with dashed outlines marking selected solution regions and color scales encoding the corresponding component objective. Panels E--F summarize the fitted parameter landscape and do not establish biological subtypes.}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:iteration1-invivo-resource-regimes-landscape}{{4}{11}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Joint fits distinguish model-supported context differences.} \textbf  {(A)} Joint-fit design. Six \textit  {in vivo} landscape-informed warm starts, spanning three clusters and two subclusters per cluster, were paired with the same best separate \textit  {in vitro} fit (seed 10); each pair was optimized from 500 numerical seeds. The matched tumor and culture likelihoods used one mechanistic model, while all 14 overlapping active biological parameters received context-specific values reconstructed around a common transformed-scale center and regularized with a Welsch soft-coupling penalty ($\sigma =0.65$, $c=0.4$).}}{12}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:iteration1-joint-context-differences}{{5}{12}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Joint fits distinguish model-supported context differences (continued).} \textbf  {(B)} Pair-level parameter-ratio map. Each cell is the median natural-scale \textit  {in vivo}/\textit  {in vitro} ratio across the 500 numerical seeds for one warm-start pair. Colors encode $\log _2$ ratios; the response-class annotations identify the separate-fit fixed-O$_2$ curve category associated with each \textit  {in vivo} warm start. Because every pair shares the same \textit  {in vitro} seed-10 anchor, these pair labels are descriptive and do not define independent experimental groups.}}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Joint fits distinguish model-supported context differences (continued).} \textbf  {(C)} Directional consensus across the six warm-start pairs. For each parameter, pair-median ratios were classified as lower \textit  {in vivo} (ratio $<1/1.1$), approximately coupled ($1/1.1$ to $1.1$), or higher \textit  {in vivo} (ratio $>1.1$). Fractions therefore use the six warm-start pairs---not the 3,000 optimizer runs or biological specimens---as the summarization unit.}}{14}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Joint fits distinguish model-supported context differences (continued).} \textbf  {(D)} Pair-specific parameter ratios. Points show medians and horizontal intervals show the 5th--95th percentiles across 500 optimizer seeds within each warm-start pair. These intervals quantify numerical optimizer-start spread; they are not confidence intervals and the seeds are not biological replicates.}}{15}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Fixed-O$_2$ fits occupy distinct oxygen--CIN--ploidy regimes.} \textbf  {(A)} Analytical dominant-eigenvector ploidy curves from 500 separate \textit  {in vivo} fits were grouped by regression-smoothed response shape; class sizes are shown above each panel, individual fits are colored, and the black curve summarizes each class. \textbf  {(B)} For each seed and fixed-O$_2$ value, the dominant eigenvector $v_N$ was used to calculate the population-average per-chromosome missegregation probability, $\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _N v_N p_{\mathrm  {mis}}(O_2,N)$. Occupied bins containing at least three fitted solutions are colored by median dominant mean ploidy; solid and dashed black contours mark 2N and 4N, respectively. The white dotted contour marks bins in which more than half of solutions have a spectral gap below 0.005, and unsupported bins are left blank. This panel is a projection of fitted solutions, not an independent oxygen-by-CIN intervention surface.}}{16}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:iteration1-o2-linked-model-selection}{{6}{16}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Fixed-O$_2$ fits occupy distinct oxygen--CIN--ploidy regimes (continued).} \textbf  {(C)} Left, the 500 separate \textit  {in vivo} fits are positioned in a t-SNE embedding of prior-scaled fitted parameters and colored by fixed-O$_2$ response class; black rings mark the lowest 10\% of objective values. Right, objective distributions are shown for all response classes, with the dotted line marking the same lowest-decile cutoff. Several common response classes and high-quality fits overlap in parameter space and objective value, and no single response class uniquely contains the best-fitting solutions. The overlay diagnoses remaining model uncertainty rather than selecting one curve class a priori.}}{17}{}\protected@file@percent }
